ed. Alternatively, **All Chows** (worth 2 points) is achievable.\n\nBut most promising is preserving either **B2-B3-B4** or **B7-B8-B9** as solid chows, and trying to pair with another chow and a pair.\n\nYou lack a pair entirely at the moment. The only duplicate is B3 (two copies). So B3 is a potential pair base.\n\nThus, safest strategy is to keep B3 as potential pair, preserve one strong chow (preferably B7-B8-B9, since higher numbers are often more valuable in scoring patterns like Upper Tiles), and break the weaker end \u2014 perhaps discard B2 or B4.\n\nBut note: you have only one B2 and one B4. To complete B2-B3-B4, you need both. With two B3s, you can afford to wait.\n\nAlternatively, focus on **B7-B8-B9** \u2014 all singles, so fragile. Losing any one kills it.\n\nTherefore, prioritize protecting **B7-B8-B9** over B2-B3-B4 because:\n- It\u2019s rarer (only one of each),\n- Completes a high-end chow,\n- Could contribute to **Upper Tiles** (if other melds align) or **Pure Straight** if combined with mid/low chows.\n\nAlso, you already have a middle-range chow in T3-T4-T5.\n\nSo better to break the low Bamboo side.\n\nNow consider discards:\n\n- **Play B2**: Breaks B2-B3-B4, but keeps B3-B4 and still two B3s. Acceptable.\n- **Play B4**: Same effect.\n- **Play T8/T9**: Safe-ish, but terminals can help others\u2019 Outside Hands.\n- **Play J2**: Loses a dragon, but no current pung. Low impact.\n- **Play B3**: Dangerous \u2014 you have two! This reduces redundancy and potential pair. Avoid.\n\nBest discard is **B4** \u2014 it slightly weakens the lower chow, but preserves B2+B3 as possible start of sequence, while focusing on completing B7-B8-B9 and using T3-T4-T5, then drawing into a pair.\n\nWait \u2014 you have **T4** listed twice? Wait: your hand is [T4:1; ...], and you already used T4 in the meld [T3,T4,T5]. So that T4 is already melded. Why is it in your hand?\n\nWait: inconsistency.\n\nLet\u2019s recheck:\n\n> Your hand tiles are: [T4: 1; T8: 1; T9: 1; B2: