x2, B4, T4, J2\n\nWe must discard one.\n\nB2 is weakest \u2014 only useful in [B2,B3,B4], which is a low pattern (Lower Tiles worth 24, but competes with Upper).\n\nBut Lower Tiles requires all 1,2,3 \u2014 we have B2,B3,B4 \u2014 B4 is not 1-3? B4 is 4 \u2014 invalid.\n\n[B2,B3,B4] is not a Lower Tile hand.\n\nLower Tiles: only 1,2,3.\n\nWe have no B1, so cannot form any chow starting with B1.\n\nSo [B2,B3,B4] is not eligible for Lower Tiles.\n\nSimilarly, no T1,T2,T3 except in meld.\n\nSo **[B2,B3,B4] is just a normal chow, not part of a high-scoring pattern**.\n\nWhereas [B7,B8,B9] is part of Upper Tiles (24 pts) or Full Flush (24 pts) if we go Bamboo-only.\n\nFull Flush in Bamboo? We have many B-tiles: B2,B3x2,B4,B7,B8,B9 \u2014 8 tiles. With [B7,B8,B9] and [B2,B3,B4] and pair, possible.\n\nBut we also have T and J tiles \u2014 would need to eliminate them.\n\nBut T8,T9 are high value.\n\nStill, Full Flush is 24 points, same as Upper Tiles.\n\nBut Upper Tiles allows honors, so easier.\n\nBut we have J2 \u2014 single honor.\n\nIf we can get rid of J2 and focus on high suits, better.\n\nNow, compare discards:\n\n- **Play J2**: Safe? Possibly. No one has shown interest. Unseen:4 \u2192 fresh tile. But dragon \u2014 sometimes collected.\n\n- **Play B2**: Only use is [B2,B3,B4]. But if we keep B3,B4, we can still hope to draw B5 or B1 (but B1 all gone) \u2014 no, cannot form anything without B1 or B5.\n\nWith B3,B4, need B2 or B5 or B1/B6.\n\nBut B2 is here \u2014 if we keep B3,B4, we need B2 or B5 for chow.\n\nBut B5:3 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nBut [B3,B4,B5] is better than [B2,B3,B4] because higher.\n\nSo keeping B3,B4 gives flexibility.\n\nDiscarding B2 now loses nothing \u2014 we can still form [B3,B4,B5] later.\n\nWhereas if we discard B3, we lose a key tile.\n\nSo **B2 is the most disposable**.\n\nSimilarly, T4 is part of mid-suit, less valuable.\n\nBut T4 could be used in [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but we already have a T-chow.\n\nNot essential.\n\nBut between B2 and