posed), and [J2,J2,J2] via Peng (exposed). So both are exposed.\n\nThus, aiming for **Melded Hand (6 pts)** is possible \u2014 meaning all melds come from other players, and winning tile is claimed. That fits your style.\n\nBut currently stuck with many non-paired singles.\n\nBest move: eliminate a tile that is most likely to be harmless.\n\nLet\u2019s compare danger:\n- **B9**: only one unseen \u2192 extremely dangerous. Do not play.\n- **T8**: T9=1 left, T7=4 left \u2192 possible T7-T8-T9 chow, but no one has declared such a sequence. Next player has mostly W and F melds. Across has B and F. Previous has concealed kongs and J3 pung. Risk moderate.\n- **B8**: similar logic. B7-B8-B9 possible, but B9 scarce. Still, avoid.\n- **W8**: W9 gone, W7=1 left. W7-W8-W9 nearly impossible now. Safe.\n- **T5**: middle tile, used in your own chi. Others may have T4/T6 issues, but T4 is exhausted (0 left). So T5 cannot form chow unless someone has T3+T4 or T6+T7. T4=0 \u2192 so T3+T4+T5 impossible now. Similarly, T4+T5+T6 impossible. So T5 can't complete any chow! Thus, **T5 is completely safe**.\n    - Why? To make a chow with T5, need either T3+T4, T4+T5, T6+T7, etc. But T4=0, so no one can have T4. Hence, **no legal chow can include T5 anymore**.\n    - Also, no pung possible unless someone has four T5s \u2014 only one T5 remains (you hold it), so impossible.\n\nTherefore, **T5 is the safest discard** \u2014 cannot help anyone complete a chow or pung.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding **T5** is optimal because the tile **T4 is exhausted (0 remaining)**, making it impossible for any opponent to use T5 in a chow (as it requires adjacent numbers). Additionally, there are insufficient copies of T5 for a pung/kong (only one exists now), so it poses no winning risk. This makes T5 a perfectly safe discard.\n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
